[THOUGHT] The consultation has reached the 10-turn limit. Key findings include:  
- 3-week fatigue without B symptoms, sleep disturbance, mood changes, or thyroid symptoms;  
- Marked thrombocytosis (700,000/μL) with abnormal platelets on smear;  
- Low serum ferritin, confirming iron deficiency;  
- No reported GI bleeding (melena/hematochezia);  
- JAK2 and CALR results still pending but not yet returned.  

While iron deficiency explains both fatigue and reactive thrombocytosis, the abnormal platelet morphology is atypical for purely reactive causes and raises concern for an underlying clonal disorder — especially essential thrombocythemia — possibly unmasked or coexisting with iron deficiency. However, with no further turns available and insufficient data to definitively distinguish reactive vs. clonal etiology, the most evidence-supported working diagnosis is **iron deficiency anemia**, given the low ferritin and fatigue, with thrombocytosis as a likely reactive phenomenon. Yet per instructions, I must not diagnose — only conclude data collection.

[FINISH]

